      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Car Insurance Rates</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the difference between full coverage and minimum liability? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            State minimum liability covers only the injuries and property damage you cause to others in an at-fault accident. It provides zero dollars for your own vehicle repair or medical treatment. Full coverage adds Collision coverage (damage to your car from an accident) and Comprehensive coverage (theft, vandalism, hail, fire, animal collisions), protecting your financial investment regardless of fault.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Will checking car insurance rates hurt my credit score? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. When insurance companies check your credit report to generate a quote, they perform a "soft inquiry" (soft pull). Soft inquiries have zero impact on your FICO or VantageScore credit scores and are invisible to lenders.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">How far back do car insurance companies look at my driving record? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Most insurance carriers review the past 3 to 5 years (36 to 60 months) of your Motor Vehicle Record for moving violations and at-fault claims. Major infractions like DUIs may impact your insurance eligibility and surcharges for up to 5 to 7 years depending on state insurance statutes.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can an insurance company raise my rates if an accident wasn’t my fault? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            In many states (such as California and Oklahoma), state law strictly prohibits carriers from imposing premium surcharges for accidents where you were not at fault. However, in certain no-fault states, insurers may adjust overall territory loss tiers or remove a "claims-free" discount if you file a claim under your own Personal Injury Protection (PIP) or collision policy.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

